 "asr_text": "One typo cost a Tokyo bank 40 billion yen, and it sold shares that didn't even exist. December 8, 2005. A small company called JCOM starts trading on the Tokyo Stock Exchange. It has only about 14 ,500 shares. At Mizuho Securities, a trader gets a simple job. Sell one share for 610 ,000 yen. He types 610 ,000 shares for one yen. That's 41 times more shares than the whole company has. But how do you sell something that doesn't exist? They press cancel. Nothing. Again, nothing. The price crashes to the floor, and all over Japan, buyers grab the cheap shares. One 27 -year -old with an online account makes over 2 billion yen. You see, a stock exchange is like a giant notice board. You pin up selling, and it doesn't stop to check if you really have it. And the cancel button? The exchange's own system had a bug. Mizuho bought most shares back, but over 96 ,000 of them never existed, so it paid cash instead. The bill? About 40 billion yen. Years later, a court ruled the exchange had to pay part of it. The typo took one second. The fight over it took eight years.",
